\margin{[$\downarrow$}\over{\illegible{1}}{25}.  A fortiori,  coming back to the intriguing question of \xout{which} carac-%
terizing the algebraic structure species suitable for describing homoto-%
py types in the usual homotopy category (Hot),  \xout{if M is the} and for
recovering (Hot) as a category of fractions of the category M of all
set-theoretic re\xout{a}alizations of this species,  it becomes clear now that
we cannot hope reasonably for a ``natural'' topology on M,  distinguished
among all others,  giving rise to the wished-for functor
\[ (*) \qquad M \to (\mathrm{Hot}) \]
in the way contemplated earlier (\deleted{page 19 ff}\inserted{section 23}).  Here it occu\over{s}{r}s to me
that anyhow,  if concerned mainly with defining the functor (*),  we
should consider that two topologies T,T' on M such that $T \hand{\geqslant} T'$  and
hence giving rise to a morphism of topoi
\[ (**) \qquad M_T^{\hand{\sim}} \to M_{T'}^{\hand{\sim}} \]
(the \deleted{inverse}\inserted{direct} image functor associated to this morphism being the natural
inclusion functor\xout{s}\hand{,} when considering T\deleted{'}-sheaves as particular cases of
T\hand{'}-sheaves) are ``\uline{equivalent}''  -  maybe we should rather say ``\uline{Hot-equiva-}%
\uline{lent}'' -  if for any  object A in M,  the induced morphism
\margin{\deleted{\illegible{1}}}
\[ (M_T^{\hand{\sim}})_{/A} \to (M_{T'}^{\hand{\sim}})_{/A} \]
\margin{\deleted{\illegible{1}}}
is a homotopy equivalence.  This can be viewed as an intrinsic property
of the morphism of topoi (**), of a type rather familiar I guess to
people used to the dial\deleted{\unsure{l}}ectics of \hand{\'{}}etale cohomology,  where a very simi-%
lar notion was met and given the name of a ``globally acyclic morphism''.
The suitable name here would be ``globally\xout{ acyclic} aspheric morphism''
which is a reinforcement of the former,  in the sense of being expressib%
le in terms of isomorphism\xout{s} relations in cohomology with \inserted{arbitrary} coefficient\xout{s} \inserted{sheaves}
on the base,  \uline{including} \uline{non-commutative} coefficient sheaves\hand{(\unsure{*}\textsuperscript{22})}.The rela-%
tion just introduced between two topologies T,T' on a category M makes
sense for any M  (irresp\xout{p}ective of the particular way M was introduced
here),  it is not yet an equivalence relation though  -  so why not
introduce the equivalence relation it generates,  and call this ``Hot-%
equivalence'' -  unless we find a still coarser,  and cleverer notion
of equivalence,  deserving this name.  The point which,  one feels now,
should be developped,  is that this notion of equivalence should be
the coarsest we can find out,  and which still implies that to any \inserted{Hot-}equi-%
valence class of topologies on M there should be canonically associated
a functor  (*) ,  which should essentially \unsure{he|be} ``the'' common value of
all the similar functors,  associated to the topologies T within \deleted{this}
\margin{(22) * For a proper map of paracompact spaces, this condition \unsure{means that the fibers are} ``\unsure{aspheric}'' \unsure{(``weakly contractible'' in the Čech sense)}}

