with respect to the object F of the topos $A^\wedge$) .  \xout{We have named the proper-}
\xout{ty of the in question for}  More generally,  for a map $F \to G$ in $A^\wedge$, the
property for this map of being $\underline{W}_A$-aspheric is a property for the corres-%
ponding map in (Cat)
\[ A_{/F} \longrightarrow A_{/G} \qquad , \]
namely the property we called  \uline{weak} \uline{$\underline{W}$-asphericity}  yesterday (page 161),
as we stated then in the prop. page 162.  An equivalent way of expressing
this is by saying that for $F \to G$ to be $\underline{W}_A$-aspheric  i.e. to be "univer-%
sally in $\underline{W}_A$ ",  it is enough to check this for base changes  $G' \to G$
with G' an object a in A,  namely that the corresponding map
\[ Fx_G a \longrightarrow a \]
in $A^\wedge$should be in $\underline{W}_A$ (for any a in A\inserted{and map $a \to G$}),  which amounts to saying that
$Fx_{G\unsure{\xout{x}}}a$  is $\underline{W}$-aspheric  for any a in A and map $a \to G$.

With notations and terminology quite clear in mind,  we may start
retelling once again the test category story !

\hand{A)} \hand{\uline{Total\deleted{ly} asphericity.}}
Before starting,  just one important pre-test notion to recall,
namely total asphericity,  summed up in the

\uline{Proposition 1}   The following conditions on A are equivalent:

(i)  The product \deleted{of} in $A^\wedge$ of any two objects of A is $\underline{W}$-aspheric .

(i') Every object in A is $\underline{W}_A$-aspheric over the final element $e_{A^\wedge}$.

(ii) The product of two $\underline{W}$-aspheric objects \inserted{in $A^\wedge$} is again $\underline{W}$-aspheric.

(ii')  Any $\underline{W}$-aspheric object of $A^\wedge$ is $\underline{W}_A$ -aspheric over $e_{A^\wedge}$ .

This is \deleted{just} a tautology,  in terms of what was just said.  Condition
